# An ALLOWLIST, not a denylist, and deliberately so.
#
# The root .gitignore already covers `.env`, which is the file people actually
# worry about. But it is a denylist: `git check-ignore secrets.yaml`,
# `kek.txt`, `admin-key.json`, `backup.sql` — none of them match anything, and
# every one of them is a plausible name for something an operator drops in this
# directory while standing the service up. This repository is public and the
# service holds the LiteLLM admin key. Once is enough: an admin key has already
# shipped inside an installer.
#
# So: ignore everything, then name what may be committed. A new file is
# invisible to git until somebody adds it here on purpose, which is exactly the
# moment to think about whether it contains a secret.
*

!.gitignore
!docker-compose.yml
!Dockerfile
!Caddyfile
!Makefile
!.env.example
!README.md
!migrations/
!migrations/**
